WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.400
Movie Dark Matter, The Great Unknown

00:03.400 --> 00:08.560
In the vast expanse of space there lies a mystery, unseen and unheard, yet it shapes

00:08.560 --> 00:10.040
our universe.

00:10.040 --> 00:12.940
This enigma, my friends, is dark matter.

00:12.940 --> 00:17.560
It's an elusive character in the cosmic drama, a phantom presence that neither reflects nor

00:17.560 --> 00:22.720
emits light, making it invisible to our eyes and even our most advanced detection technologies,

00:22.720 --> 00:24.920
yet its footprints are everywhere.

00:24.920 --> 00:28.640
You see, dark matter can't be seen or touched, but we know it's there.

00:28.640 --> 00:29.720
It's like the wind.

00:29.720 --> 00:32.880
We can't see it, but we can see its effects.

00:32.880 --> 00:36.360
It's the gravitational glue that holds galaxies together.

00:36.360 --> 00:42.480
It bends the path of light and silently molds the universe's large-scale structure.

00:42.480 --> 00:47.100
It's a puzzle that has intrigued scientists for decades, a cosmic conundrum that continues

00:47.100 --> 00:49.240
to elude our understanding.

00:49.240 --> 00:54.680
This enigmatic substance, this unseen force, this invisible shaper of cosmos and galaxies,

00:54.680 --> 00:56.740
is what we call dark matter.

00:56.740 --> 01:02.220
This is dark matter, the great unknown, the silent shaper of our universe.

01:02.220 --> 01:06.420
Imagine a giant invisible, yet it has the power to move galaxies.

01:06.420 --> 01:08.620
This is the reality of dark matter.

01:08.620 --> 01:09.780
It's a bit like the wind.

01:09.780 --> 01:13.020
We can't see it, we can't touch it, but we can see its effects.

01:13.020 --> 01:16.100
We can see the trees swaying, the leaves rustling.

01:16.100 --> 01:17.700
Dark matter is like that.

01:17.700 --> 01:21.660
We can't see it, we can't touch it, but we can see its impact on the universe around

01:21.660 --> 01:22.660
us.

01:22.660 --> 01:25.620
Dark matter is the invisible giant of the cosmos.

01:25.620 --> 01:31.820
It's a mysterious, unseen force that makes up around 85% of the matter in the universe.

01:31.820 --> 01:37.380
Despite being invisible and untouchable, it has a profound impact on the universe.

01:37.380 --> 01:40.700
It's the glue that holds galaxies together.

01:40.700 --> 01:43.580
Without dark matter, galaxies would fly apart.

01:43.580 --> 01:48.460
The stars and planets that make up these galaxies would be scattered across the universe, like

01:48.460 --> 01:50.460
marbles spilled from a bag.

01:50.460 --> 01:54.420
Dark matter also plays a crucial role in the formation of galaxies.

01:54.420 --> 01:57.140
It's a bit like the scaffolding of the universe.

01:57.140 --> 02:00.340
Without this scaffolding, galaxies wouldn't form.

02:00.340 --> 02:03.380
The universe would be a chaotic, structureless mess.

02:03.380 --> 02:09.260
But thanks to dark matter, galaxies form beautiful intricate patterns, like cosmic ballet dancers

02:09.260 --> 02:11.020
spinning in the dark.

02:11.020 --> 02:15.780
But the impact of dark matter goes beyond the formation and movement of galaxies.

02:15.780 --> 02:18.820
It also affects the expansion of the universe.

02:18.820 --> 02:24.220
Dark matter acts like a cosmic break, slowing down the expansion of the universe.

02:24.220 --> 02:28.680
Without dark matter, the universe would be expanding at a much faster rate.

02:28.680 --> 02:33.340
Dark matter is a silent giant, moving unseen through the universe.

02:33.340 --> 02:36.820
It's a bit like a puppet master pulling the strings of the cosmos.

02:36.820 --> 02:41.580
We can't see the puppet master, but we can see the puppets dancing.

02:41.580 --> 02:47.100
Invisible yet omnipresent, dark matter silently directs the cosmic dance of galaxies.

02:47.100 --> 02:53.060
It's the invisible giant, the great unknown, the silent conductor of the cosmic symphony.

02:53.060 --> 02:57.740
And yet, despite its profound impact on the universe, we know so little about it.

02:57.740 --> 03:02.420
It's a mystery that continues to perplex scientists and captivate the imagination.

03:02.420 --> 03:06.820
It's the great unknown, the invisible giant of the universe.

03:06.820 --> 03:11.380
What if you knew that what we see, touch, and feel makes up only 5% of the universe,

03:11.380 --> 03:14.100
the rest is dark matter and dark energy?

03:14.100 --> 03:18.380
In the grand cosmic play, everything that seems tangible and familiar to us, including

03:19.380 --> 03:24.340
stars, galaxies, and even the interstellar dust, is just a minuscule part.

03:24.340 --> 03:30.180
This ordinary matter, all the atoms and molecules that we know of, is a mere 5% of the universe.

03:30.180 --> 03:32.100
Now let that sink in for a moment.

03:32.100 --> 03:33.100
5%.

03:33.100 --> 03:37.780
That's like being in a colossal mansion and only having access to one small closet.

03:37.780 --> 03:40.180
So what about the rest?

03:40.180 --> 03:45.740
The mysterious 95%, well, it's made up of dark matter and dark energy.

03:45.740 --> 03:49.460
Dark matter alone accounts for about 27% of the universe.

03:49.460 --> 03:54.100
It's this invisible yet massive presence that holds galaxies together.

03:54.100 --> 03:58.660
Without it, the cosmic structures that we see today wouldn't exist.

03:58.660 --> 04:02.220
The stars would scatter and galaxies would fly apart.

04:02.220 --> 04:06.500
Dark matter is like the hidden framework, the unseen scaffolding that keeps the universe

04:06.500 --> 04:07.500
intact.

04:07.500 --> 04:11.560
It doesn't interact with light or any other form of electromagnetic radiation, making

04:11.560 --> 04:15.020
it utterly invisible and incredibly difficult to detect.

04:15.020 --> 04:17.460
But its gravitational effects are undeniable.

04:17.460 --> 04:20.620
They are the silent whispers that hint at its existence.

04:20.620 --> 04:24.560
The remaining 68% of the universe is made up of dark energy.

04:24.560 --> 04:29.260
This is an even more elusive entity that is believed to be responsible for the accelerated

04:29.260 --> 04:31.180
expansion of the universe.

04:31.180 --> 04:36.600
It's like a cosmic push, defying gravity and causing galaxies to drift further apart.

04:36.600 --> 04:42.060
So in this vast cosmic sea, dark matter and dark energy are the unseen majority.

04:42.060 --> 04:47.060
They are the silent, invisible giants that shape the universe and dictate its destiny.

04:47.060 --> 04:52.580
They might be elusive, but their influence is far-reaching, their presence undeniable.

04:52.580 --> 04:57.260
In this grand cosmic ballet, it's not the visible stars and galaxies that hold the center

04:57.260 --> 04:58.260
stage.

04:58.260 --> 05:01.300
It's the unseen, the elusive, the mysterious.

05:01.300 --> 05:06.740
It's the dark matter and dark energy that truly carry the weight of the universe.

05:07.620 --> 05:12.780
The unseen majority carries the weight of our universe on its elusive shoulders.

05:12.780 --> 05:16.820
Scientists like detectives are on a relentless hunt, piecing together the clues left by this

05:16.820 --> 05:18.780
invisible entity.

05:18.780 --> 05:23.820
In the cosmic game of hide-and-seek, dark matter is the elusive quarry.

05:23.820 --> 05:27.820
Our scientific sleuths are pulling out all the stops, employing the most cutting-edge

05:27.820 --> 05:31.380
methods to detect this mysterious substance.

05:31.380 --> 05:36.660
Picture a vast field of cosmic detectives, armed with telescopes instead of magnifying

05:36.660 --> 05:42.980
glasses, pouring over the vast expanses of the universe in search of an elusive suspect.

05:42.980 --> 05:46.500
One of the key tools in their arsenal is gravitational lensing.

05:46.500 --> 05:51.300
This technique exploits the fact that massive objects, like dark matter, warp spacetime

05:51.300 --> 05:54.100
around them, causing light to bend.

05:54.100 --> 05:58.740
By observing how the light from distant galaxies is distorted, scientists can map out where

05:58.740 --> 06:02.900
dark matter is hiding, but it's not all plain sailing.

06:02.900 --> 06:06.140
Detecting dark matter is akin to trying to catch smoke with a net.

06:06.140 --> 06:10.180
It doesn't interact with light or ordinary matter, making it incredibly tricky to pin

06:10.180 --> 06:11.180
down.

06:11.180 --> 06:14.180
But this hasn't deterred our intrepid explorers.

06:14.180 --> 06:18.460
They've developed ingenious methods to overcome this hurdle, such as building detectors deep

06:18.460 --> 06:22.900
underground to shield them from cosmic rays, enabling them to catch the faint signals

06:22.900 --> 06:24.260
of dark matter.

06:24.260 --> 06:28.140
The journey has been filled with challenges, but each one surmounted brings us closer to

06:28.140 --> 06:29.180
the truth.

06:29.180 --> 06:35.020
The discovery of the Higgs boson, often dubbed the God particle, was a major milestone, opening

06:35.020 --> 06:38.420
up new avenues for understanding dark matter.

06:38.420 --> 06:40.900
And the advancements keep coming.

06:40.900 --> 06:44.480
Scientists are now exploring the intriguing possibility that dark matter might not be

06:44.480 --> 06:50.700
a single entity, but a family of particles, each with their own unique properties.

06:50.700 --> 06:54.500
This opens up a whole new chapter in the dark matter detective story.

06:54.500 --> 06:56.060
The stakes are high.

06:56.060 --> 07:00.940
Unlocking the secrets of dark matter could revolutionize our understanding of the universe,

07:00.940 --> 07:04.780
reshaping our comprehension of everything from the smallest particles to the largest

07:04.780 --> 07:06.740
galaxies.

07:06.740 --> 07:12.060
The hunt is on, and the clues are being assembled, inching us closer to uncovering the truth

07:12.060 --> 07:13.620
of dark matter.

07:13.620 --> 07:17.420
So why should we care about this invisible, distant matter, you may ask?

07:17.420 --> 07:22.000
Well, the answer lies in our inherent curiosity and thirst for knowledge.

07:22.000 --> 07:27.060
Dark matter, though elusive and mysterious, is a major part of the cosmos.

07:27.060 --> 07:32.580
It's like the unseen backbone of the universe, holding galaxies together and playing a crucial

07:32.660 --> 07:35.700
role in the grand cosmic ballet.

07:35.700 --> 07:40.420
Unraveling the secrets of dark matter is not just a scientific endeavor, it's a journey

07:40.420 --> 07:42.480
of self-discovery.

07:42.480 --> 07:47.580
As we probe the depths of the universe, we also delve into the depths of our own existence.

07:47.580 --> 07:51.780
What can be more exciting than understanding the very fabric of the universe and our role

07:51.780 --> 07:53.060
within it?

07:53.060 --> 07:58.360
Moreover, the study of dark matter could potentially revolutionize technology and science, just

07:58.360 --> 08:02.300
as quantum mechanics has given us lasers and transistors.

08:02.300 --> 08:05.880
The implications are immense and deeply personal.

08:05.880 --> 08:09.780
Dark matter might be distant and untouchable, but its understanding brings us closer to

08:09.780 --> 08:12.580
comprehending our universe and our place in it.

08:12.580 --> 08:17.420
As we stand on the precipice of discovery, dark matter remains our final frontier.

08:17.420 --> 08:22.800
An enigma, a puzzle, a potential key to unlocking the deepest secrets of the cosmos.

08:22.800 --> 08:27.660
Our journey into the abyss of the unknown has only just begun, and the path before us

08:27.660 --> 08:31.000
is as enthralling as it is daunting.

08:31.000 --> 08:33.680
Consider the future of dark matter research.

08:33.680 --> 08:38.760
It's a field brimming with promise, teeming with questions waiting to be answered.

08:38.760 --> 08:43.040
Will we find the elusive particles that make up dark matter, or will we discover that our

08:43.040 --> 08:46.760
understanding of the universe needs to be fundamentally altered?

08:46.760 --> 08:50.440
The prospects are as limitless as the cosmos itself.

08:50.440 --> 08:56.640
This isn't a path for the fainthearted, it's a journey for the bold, the curious, the relentless.

08:56.640 --> 09:00.340
The challenges are immense, but so too are the rewards.

09:00.340 --> 09:05.440
To comprehend dark matter is to potentially understand the very fabric of our universe.

09:05.440 --> 09:10.760
To grasp the invisible threads that weave together galaxies, stars, and planets.

09:10.760 --> 09:12.720
Imagine the possibilities.

09:12.720 --> 09:16.840
If we can harness the secrets of dark matter, we might revolutionize not just physics but

09:16.840 --> 09:18.200
all of science.

09:18.200 --> 09:23.240
We could redefine our understanding of energy, gravity, perhaps even time itself.

09:23.240 --> 09:27.080
We might be on the cusp of a new era of scientific enlightenment.

09:27.080 --> 09:29.640
But let's not forget the human element in all of this.

09:29.640 --> 09:34.080
Dark matter research isn't just about particles and galaxies, it's about us, about our place

09:34.080 --> 09:35.580
in the cosmos.

09:35.580 --> 09:39.680
It's a quest for knowledge, a testament to our enduring curiosity, and our relentless

09:39.680 --> 09:41.160
pursuit of understanding.

09:41.160 --> 09:46.040
Sure, we might stumble, we might encounter dead ends and false starts, but that's the

09:46.040 --> 09:48.320
nature of exploration, isn't it?

09:48.320 --> 09:51.880
Every step, every misstep brings us closer to understanding.

09:51.880 --> 09:56.200
It's a journey well worth the effort, the trials, the tribulations.

09:56.200 --> 10:00.740
As we journey into this great unknown, the mystery of dark matter awaits inviting us

10:00.740 --> 10:02.920
to unravel the secrets of our universe.

